<p>The streets around the historic downtown include older wood-frame and masonry homes, some with steep roofs, porches and chimneys. Over the years these roofs often pick up patchwork repairs, mismatched shingles and flashing that was sealed with caulk instead of replaced. A careful <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> that replaces worn flashing correctly often solves a leak that years of patching never did.</p>

<h3>Newer neighborhoods toward the lake and the edges of town</h3>
<p>Belton has grown outward, and many newer homes have complex roof lines with multiple valleys and roof-to-wall joints. These are spots where hail damage and wind-lifted shingles show up first. If you live in a newer subdivision, check with your HOA about shingle color or material rules before a replacement.</p>

<h3>Rental and student housing</h3>
<p>With a university in town, Belton has a fair share of rental homes. Landlords often want a clear written assessment they can review remotely. Our photo documentation and written estimates work well for owners who cannot be on site.</p>

<h2>Weather Exposure in Belton</h2>
<p>Belton gets the same Central Texas pattern as Temple: severe storms with hail and damaging winds most often in spring, a second storm season in the fall, long summers in the upper 90s and higher, and the occasional hard freeze. Homes on higher ground or open lots toward the lake can take more direct wind. Mature trees in older neighborhoods add another issue: limbs that rub shingles, drop debris into valleys and clog gutters.</p>

